{
  "dom_id": "step-q86650",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}-\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{4}=1.$",
  "worked_solution": "The standard equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nFor a horizontal hyperbola, the equations of the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny-k=\\pm \\frac{b}{a}(x-h).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n(h,k)=(0,-2),\\,a=\\sqrt{25}=5,\\,b=\\sqrt{4}=2.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the asymptotes are given by\n\n$$\ny+2=\\pm \\frac{2}{5}x.\n$$",
